The approach is a run-aware fast path with a multi-table fallback. I could not compile or run it, so it is untested.

- **Fast path:** `c` is the current run byte, and `pat` is `c` repeated across 8 bytes. Each iteration loads 64 bytes as eight 64-bit words and XORs each with `pat`. If the OR of the results is zero, the whole block is `c`, so a local `run` counter goes up by 64 and no table is touched.
- **Mixed blocks:** `run` is flushed into the tables and the block is counted byte by byte. It uses four separate 256-entry `uint64_t` tables, so back-to-back increments of the same byte don't serialise on one store-to-load dependency. Afterwards `c` and `pat` are reset to the block's last byte, since the run most likely continues from there.
- **Tail and output:** the last bytes, fewer than 64, are counted with the same tables, and any pending `run` is added. `counts[b]` is then the sum of the four tables.
- **Edge cases:** `n == 0` falls through to all zeros. `counts` is fully overwritten, so its initial garbage doesn't matter.
- **Memory:** the four tables are 8 KiB on the stack, well under the 4 MiB limit.

<answer>
```c
#include <arm_neon.h>

void byte_histogram(const uint8_t *data, size_t n, uint64_t counts[256]) {
    uint64_t t[4][256];
    memset(t, 0, sizeof(t));
    size_t i = 0;
    unsigned c = 0;
    uint64_t pat = 0;
    uint64_t run = 0;
    if (n >= 64) {
        c = data[0];
        pat = c * 0x0101010101010101ULL;
    }
    while (i + 64 <= n) {
        const uint8_t *p = data + i;
        uint64_t w[8];
        memcpy(w, p, 64);
        uint64_t x = (w[0] ^ pat) | (w[1] ^ pat) | (w[2] ^ pat) | (w[3] ^ pat) |
                     (w[4] ^ pat) | (w[5] ^ pat) | (w[6] ^ pat) | (w[7] ^ pat);
        if (__builtin_expect(x == 0, 1)) {
            run += 64;
        } else {
            t[0][c] += run;
            run = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < 64; j += 4) {
                t[0][p[j]]++;
                t[1][p[j + 1]]++;
                t[2][p[j + 2]]++;
                t[3][p[j + 3]]++;
            }
            c = p[63];
            pat = c * 0x0101010101010101ULL;
        }
        i += 64;
    }
    t[0][c] += run;
    for (; i < n; i++) {
        t[i & 3][data[i]]++;
    }
    for (int b = 0; b < 256; b++) {
        counts[b] = t[0][b] + t[1][b] + t[2][b] + t[3][b];
    }
}
```
</answer>

The approach is to find runs with NEON and add each run's length to its counter in one step, so repeated bytes never wait on a store-to-load dependency. Non-run data goes through four interleaved sub-histograms. I have not compiled or run this.

- **Run detection:** at each position, load 16 bytes and compare them with the first byte. If all 16 match, extend the run in 64-byte steps, then 16-byte steps, and locate the exact end with a count-trailing-zeros on the narrowed compare mask. A scalar loop handles the last few bytes before `n`.
- **Non-run data:** if the 16 bytes are not all equal, count them into four 256-entry `uint32_t` tables (4 KB total), spreading consecutive bytes across tables so repeated values don't serialize. A run that starts mid-block loses at most 15 bytes to the tables before it is detected at the next block.
- **Result:** run counts go into a local `uint64_t` table. At the end, `counts[x]` is that table plus the four sub-tables.
- **Memory and limits:** extra memory is about 6 KB of stack, well under the 4 MiB limit. The `uint32_t` tables are safe for the 64 MiB benchmark, but could overflow for inputs above roughly 16 GiB.
- **Edge cases:** `n == 0` and tiny sizes fall through to the scalar tail and still write all 256 counts.

<answer>
```c
#include <arm_neon.h>

static inline uint64_t k_eqmask16(uint8x16_t v, uint8x16_t d) {
    uint8x16_t eq = vceqq_u8(v, d);
    return vget_lane_u64(vreinterpret_u64_u8(vshrn_n_u16(vreinterpretq_u16_u8(eq), 4)), 0);
}

void byte_histogram(const uint8_t *data, size_t n, uint64_t counts[256]) {
    uint32_t t[4][256];
    uint64_t big[256];
    memset(t, 0, sizeof t);
    memset(big, 0, sizeof big);

    size_t i = 0;
    while (i + 16 <= n) {
        uint8_t b = data[i];
        uint8x16_t d = vdupq_n_u8(b);
        uint8x16_t v = vld1q_u8(data + i);
        uint64_t m = k_eqmask16(v, d);
        if (m == ~0ULL) {
            size_t j = i + 16;
            while (j + 64 <= n) {
                uint8x16_t e0 = vceqq_u8(vld1q_u8(data + j), d);
                uint8x16_t e1 = vceqq_u8(vld1q_u8(data + j + 16), d);
                uint8x16_t e2 = vceqq_u8(vld1q_u8(data + j + 32), d);
                uint8x16_t e3 = vceqq_u8(vld1q_u8(data + j + 48), d);
                uint8x16_t a = vandq_u8(vandq_u8(e0, e1), vandq_u8(e2, e3));
                if (vminvq_u8(a) != 0xff) break;
                j += 64;
            }
            int done = 0;
            while (j + 16 <= n) {
                uint64_t mm = k_eqmask16(vld1q_u8(data + j), d);
                if (mm != ~0ULL) {
                    j += (size_t)(__builtin_ctzll(~mm) >> 2);
                    done = 1;
                    break;
                }
                j += 16;
            }
            if (!done) {
                while (j < n && data[j] == b) j++;
            }
            big[b] += j - i;
            i = j;
        } else {
            uint64_t a, c;
            memcpy(&a, data + i, 8);
